  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Meandering frontal boundary has finally made \nits way offshore early this morning, currently draped from \nroughly Wilmington to Onslow Bay where convection is blossoming.\nBehind this boundary, seeing a modest uptick in shower activity\nas mid-level shortwave digs across the mid-Atlantic. There have\nbeen a few elevated thunderstorms riding along an elevated \nfrontal boundary with modest instability present, and this risk \nwill likely persist for a few more hours as the front moves \nlittle.\n\nThe front is likely to wobble but move little through today as \nmultiple mid-level waves traverse over the boundary in a \npersistent troughing pattern. The relative lull currently \nobserved on radar is likely to fill in later this morning as SW \nLLJ increases once again, with heaviest coverage favored south \nof Highway 70 where low-level convergence is maximized along the\nfrontal boundary. Like we saw yesterday, it is not going to be \na constant downpour but rather sct to widespread shower and iso \nthunderstorm coverage. Very moist airmass remains in place with \nPWATs nearing 2.5\" at times. Persistent cloud cover will keep \ntemps down and limit available instability, so overall rainfall \nrates will not be as intense as yesterday. Still, given the \nlikely coverage of activity some respectable rainfall \naccumulations are likely where training occurs.\n\n00Z guidance suite continues to hint at the front lifting \nslightly back north tonight and into tomorrow as yet another \nwave traverses the frontal zone. It will be a rinse and repeat \nto this morning, with a likely lull in coverage before midnight \nand then another uptick on coverage as the LLJ restrengthens. \nInstability will remain poor to marginal which should reduce max\npotential rainfall rates somewhat, but rates up to an inch an \nhour are still very much possible. Notably, the aforementioned \nwave will help to deepen the mid-level trough, and a successive \nwave Sat night will help develop a weak wave of low pressure \nthat should finally pull the frontal boundary and deeper \nmoisture offshore.\n\nOverall rainfall amounts have changed little with continued \npotential for 3-5\" in corridors where training occurs. Highest \nprobability of this is south of Highway 264 to the southern \ncoast, as well as the Outer Banks from Oregon Inlet south. In \nany robust convection, higher amounts are probable. The REFS \ncontinues to show probabilities of 40-50% for amounts over 5\" \nmainly along the southern coast south of Highway 70 for today \nand Saturday. We remain in a Slight (Level 2/4) risk of \nexcessive rainfall through Saturday, per WPC.\n\nThe Flood Watch for the northern half of the FA will be allowed\nto expire this morning. Given the persistent heavy rainfall \nsignal into the weekend, the segment for the southern half has \nbeen extended to Saturday morning and may need further \nextensions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...With Sunday and Monday trending drier with the \nlingering front finally getting carried offshore, focus then \nturns to another front approaching the eastern CONUS next mid- \nweek. This boundary appears weaker and more progressive than our\ncurrent boundary but will still provide a focus for showers and\nthunderstorms. Ahead of the boundary, strengthening \nsouthwesterly flow and building low-level thicknesses will bring\na return of oppressive heat with at least a couple days of heat\nindices approaching or exceeding 105F possible.",
